.TH MD 1
.SH NAME
md/fmt \- fill Markdown text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B md/fmt
[
.BI -l " n"
] [
.B -j
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Md/fmt
is
.IR fmt (1)
for Markdown.
It copies the given
.I files
(standard input by default)
to its standard output,
filling each paragraph, list item and block quote to a line length,
and copying the rest as it is.
.PP
What a block is comes from the Markdown parser Xenith's
.B Render
and Lucifer draw a document with,
so the text filled is the same document to them.
In particular:
.IP \(bu
List items are not joined to one another.
A wrapped item continues under the start of its text, not its marker,
a task's
.B [ ]
included.
.IP \(bu
A line that would begin another block (a word such as
.BR - ,
.BR + ,
.BR 12. ,
.B #tag
or
.B >
at its start) is not made: the word stays on the line before,
which may then be longer than
.IR n .
.IP \(bu
Code (fenced or indented), headings, tables, horizontal rules,
HTML and link reference definitions are copied unchanged.
.IP \(bu
Block quotes are filled inside their
.B >
marks, nested ones too.
.IP \(bu
A line ending in two spaces or a backslash (a hard break) still ends there.
.IP \(bu
A code span
.RB ( `...` )
is never broken, and its spaces are kept.
.IP \(bu
The spaces between two words of a line are kept as they were typed;
two lines joined are joined with one.
.PP
The options are
.TP
.BI -l " n
Output line length is
.IR n ,
including indent (default 70).
.TP
.BI -w " n
A synonym for
.BR -l .
.TP
.B -j
Do not join short lines: only fold long lines.
.PP
Empty lines are kept, and inserted between input files.
.I Md/fmt
is idempotent: it leaves already filled text unchanged.
.SH EXAMPLES
In Xenith, select a list or a paragraph of a Markdown file and execute
.IP
.EX
|md/fmt
.EE
.PP
or fill the whole file with
.B Edit ,|md/fmt
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/md/fmt.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR fmt (1)
.SH BUGS
It fills what the parser it shares with Render finds, not CommonMark:
where they differ, it follows Render.
